A: Your problem is a variant of the Partition Equitable problem in Number Theory and Combinatorics. Partition equitable sequences exists if it can be partitioned into two subsequences A and B such that the elements of A form a permutation of the first n / 2 integers and the elements of B form a permutation of the remaining n / 2 integers where n is even.

As far as I know, the problem you've described doesn't have a general solution, because it relies on absolute difference constraints. The problem becomes simpler when we allow relative difference constraints, such as allowing differences between adjacent terms.

Please note that this variant requires a more relaxed constraint (differences need not be unique), and does not guarantee existence unlike the Partition Equitable problem. 

As for your python function request, to implement your approach, we will:
- Start by sorting the array
- Then, take the smallest differences when pairing the smallest and largest values, second smallest and second largest, etc, to ensure there are no repeating differences
- To monitor this, we keep a running sum of these differences
- Finally, if our sum > 1 at any point, we'll know this sequence can't be achieved and exit the function accordingly. If we get through this process without the sum exceeding 1, we can return the array.

